WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.780
A serialization safety roadmap begins with deliberate format choice.

2
00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:13.160
Choose formats that match the trust level, interoperability needs, performance constraints, and reviewability needs of the product.

3
00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:19.580
Treat deserialization as a trust boundary because it turns external bytes or text into program state.

4
00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:25.040
Clear schemas and versioning remove ambiguity and make compatibility rules testable.

5
00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:28.680
Next, parse and validate before use.

6
00:00:28.780 --> 00:00:39.980
A safe parser builds controlled intermediate structures, validates fields and relationships, enforces safe defaults, and constructs domain objects only after checks pass.

7
00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:46.240
Limits on lengths, counts, nesting depth, memory, and time protect availability.

8
00:00:46.340 --> 00:00:52.500
Failure behavior should reject unsafe input cleanly rather than guessing or continuing with partial state.

9
00:00:52.780 --> 00:00:55.660
Then control object construction and data protection.

10
00:00:55.760 --> 00:01:04.080
Avoid raw C++ object layout, copied pointers, process-specific assumptions, and unconstrained polymorphic reconstruction.

11
00:01:04.180 --> 00:01:08.900
Rebuild ownership, lifetime, and type choices through explicit rules.

12
00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:18.120
Where the data requires it, protect integrity, authenticity, and confidentiality with approved cryptographic libraries and managed keys.

13
00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:23.120
Finally, test, review, and govern the format over time.

14
00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:31.940
Malformed-input tests, compatibility tests, defensive fuzzing, safe logging, and code review make serialization behavior measurable.

15
00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:42.920
Format changes should be reviewed as API changes because serialized data often becomes a long-lived contract between systems, versions, teams, and customers.

16
00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:45.760
The lasting habit is to make assumptions visible.

17
00:01:45.860 --> 00:01:57.460
When a format's trust level, limits, version policy, and ownership are documented, engineers can change it safely instead of rediscovering hidden behavior through production failures.
